# Review

## Summary

Most of the note's sentences are short and carry one idea. The overload clusters in the Current answer bullets and the brevity-pressure Unsettled item. The main cause is a recurring pattern: a full claim-title link is used as the grammatical subject, and a long object or contrast follows it. That leaves two long dependencies open at once. Each case can be fixed by reordering: front the qualifier, put the finding before the citing links, or turn `distinguishes X from Y` into two coordinated clauses. Only the double-quotation sentence in the brevity item needs a split, because it reports two separate results.
